    "summary": "High‑energy 1971 show with blazing Casey Jones and smoky Ramble on Rose",
    "blurb": "The set bursts with energy from a fiery Casey Jones into One More Saturday Night, while the second set delivers a scorching Ramble on Rose and tight Not Fade Away jams. Jerry’s guitar is solid despite a minor Brown Eyed Women flub, Phil lays down “lots o’ Phil” on bass, and Keith’s piano adds character throughout.",
    "review": "On December 9, 1971 the Dead took St. Louis’ Fox Theatre by storm. From the opening “Truckin'” through a raucous “Brown Eyed Women” (despite a tiny vocal slip) the band kept the momentum rolling, hitting classics like “Mr. Charlie,” “Jack Straw,” and an exuberant “Sugaree.” The first set’s high point is a scorching “Casey Jones” that segues into a rollicking “One More Saturday Night,” where Phil’s bass work earns the nickname “Lots o’ Phil.”  \nThe second set turns up the heat even further. Keith’s piano on “Ramble On Rose” is delightfully distorted, giving the song a gritty edge while Pigpen’s harmonica weaves through. A punchy “Mexicali Blues,” followed by an infectious “Sugar Magnolia,” leads into a double‑take of “Not Fade Away” that rides a rolling groove with Bill’s steady drumming. The show closes on a high note with a joyful repeat of “Not Fade Away,” leaving the audience buzzing long after the lights went down.",
